ything about it. I believe \u2014\"\n[1150] \"Listen, Petty,\" the inspector's tone had a warning note. \"Just listen to your father once! Petty, you're interested in that boy, aren't you? Is he pursuing you? Humph, I'll break his neck!\" [1151] Percy stood up, saying angrily, \"Dad!\" [1152] \"All right, all right, Sergeant,\" the old man murmured, \"don't be so stubborn. Gordon Lowe is a fine young man, and his scholarship matches yours, which is rare!\" [1153] \"I tell you, I don't love him!\" Percy called, \"Dad, you're terrible. Can't I be good to a man?\" [1154] The sergeant looked pitiful. [1155] Mr. Jules Reine stood up. \"Stop arguing. Sergeant, you're so childish. Put this paper and envelope back in the safe. We have to go to the Saxon's house.\" [1156] Chapter 12\n[1157] Good luck to me\n[1158] Traffic was heavy, and the Lincoln crawled along Fifth Avenue, Dromio sweating with impatience. But Mr. Vance seemed unruffled; he glanced at Sam and Paisin and chuckled. \"You two are a pair of bad-tempered kids. Smile!\" [1159] \"You're a pair of bad-tempered kids,\" he continued. \"What an extraordinary case! I don't think you realize how extraordinary it is.\" [1160] \"I have a headache,\" the Chief complained. [1161]\"And you, Peachum?\" [1162]\"I think\u2014\" Peachum was still watching Dromio's red back of the head, \"\u2014that those symbols mean a lot more to you than they do to us.\" [1163]The old gentleman was slightly surprised, and he leaned forward, his eyes sharp as he studied her smooth, young face. \"Perhaps so,\" he said, \"but in the past. Inspector, any progress? There's been so much going on this morning that I haven't had a chance to ask.\" [1164]\"Quite a bit,\" the inspector said wearily, \"Mr. Broude has it all down. I know you'd want to know.\" [1165]Donohue: Still missing, no trace found. [1166] Seventeen teachers: Back in Indiana. All identities checked out without suspicion. Investigation conducted with caution. Photos, features, addresses, names \u2013 listed in order. [1167] One hundred yuan note: withdrawn from a 1959 Jagger. No further information can be obtained from the serial number on the note. [1168] Blue Hats: Still no trace. [1169] The nineteenth person on the bus: No trace found. [1170] \"Inspector, is that all?\" Lestrade said, returning the report and looking somewhat disappointed, \"I thought you had sent a telegram to Scotland Yard.\" [1171] \"Never forget, do you, you old fox?\" The inspector grinned, \"Not quite, more like an elephant, eh? Yes, Scotland Yard's Trunch gave me a reply. It was like honey, arrived late yesterday. Take a look.\" [1172] He handed Reen a stack of telegrams, which the old man eagerly snatched. They watched his face, growing more serious as he read. The telegram was addressed to the inspector and read as follows:\n[1173] The Sedras are an ancient British family, dating back to the Second Crusade. Sir Hamnet Sedra was known for being Shakespeare's friend. The current Sedra is five feet eleven inches tall, weighs one hundred fifty-four pounds, has a lean build, prominent features, blue eyes, and blond hair, with no other distinguishing features. He is fifty-one years old, and his personal life is unknown.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[851] Mrs. Saxon stared at Percy with wide eyes, as if she had never seen a young woman angry in her life. Young Rowe chuckled. [852] \"Yes, Percy,\" Mr. Jury Lean said gently, \"we don't doubt Sam's astuteness, but I dare say he's wasted his effort this time. The important thing is, your 1599 J\u00e4ger has not been returned in its original form. Please check the back of the book.\" [853] His sharp eyes had already noticed something was amiss. Dr. Jot took the book from the wrapping paper and flipped it over. They immediately found out what was wrong. A 