rs. Saxon, who seemed quite satisfied with the recovery of the 1599 Jagger work, suddenly let out a startled shriek, like the sound of a steamboat's whistle. \"Klaber! This is really strange. I just thought of it. Do you know, Mr. Rain, we recently had a similar experience.\" [865] \"What happened, Mrs. Saxon?\" The old gentleman asked eagerly, \"What kind of experience?\" [866] Her three chins quivered with excitement. \"Someone stole a book from my library, Mr. Reine, and then returned it!\" [867] Clapp gave her a strange look. \"I remember. It was very strange.\" He glanced sharply at Dr. Sedley. \"Very strange indeed.\" [868] \"Clapp!\" Roe also exclaimed loudly. \"Oh, we're idiots! Of course, it must be the same person.\" [869] Mr. Reine grabbed the arm of the librarian at the Saxton Library, who recoiled in pain. \"Alright, tell me what happened, say it now! This could be a matter of great importance.\" [870] Clapp looked around mischievously. \"I forgot... about six weeks ago, I was working late in the library \u2013 of course, the Saxton Library. I was reorganizing the catalog because some books were donated to Britain. I heard a strange sound coming from one of the rooms, so I went to check it out, and to my surprise, I saw someone rummaging through a bookshelf.\" [871] \"Now we're getting somewhere,\" the Inspector said. \"What did he look like?\" [872] Klaepfer spread his bony hands, as if to warm them. \"Who knows? It was dark, and he was wearing a mask and a coat, and I only caught a glimpse of him. He heard me coming and ran out the window and escaped.\" [873] \"It was terrible at the time,\" Mrs. Saxelrod said disapprovingly. \"I'll never forget how we were all so disappointed.\" She chuckled. \"Mr. Klaepfer was hopping around like a chicken with its head cut off.\" [874] \"Hmph!\" Klaepfer retorted. \"I remember Mrs. Saxelrod wearing a big red nightgown\u2026\" The two glared at each other. Pechins had to bite his lip to keep from imagining the large