nd of wrapping paper and string?\" the sergeant asked. [894] \"About the same as these.\" [895] Renard looked thoughtfully at the Jagger cabinet and then carefully examined the 1599 edition of Jagger that the messenger had returned, examining the cut cover at least half of the bottom \u2014 the lower pastedown and the top page of the endpapers \u2014 which were curling up from the bottom. [896] \"That's strange,\" said the old gentleman, thinking aloud, and then lifted the cut page of the cover and gently opened it to the bottom. There was a rectangular cavity underneath, obviously a layer of cardboard had been cut out from under the cover. This shallow cavity was at most three inches wide and five inches long. [897] \"Did he cut that too?\" asked Dr. Jotter incredulously. [898] \"I don't think so. Dear Percy, your eyes are sharper. Do you think this strange rectangle was cut out of the cardboard?\" [899] Percy obediently walked up and said after a while, \"It was cut a long time ago, the edges are a little shiny because of the time passed. In my opinion, it's very old.\" [900] \"I think that answers your question, Dr. Jotter,\" said Rene with a smile. \"Child, why do you think they dug this rectangular cavity?\" [901] Percy smiled back. \"It's obviously meant for hiding something.\" [902] \"Hiding something!\" the librarian exclaimed. \"Absurd!\" [903] \"Doctor, doctor,\" the old actor murmured sadly. \"Why do you intellectuals always mock the correctness of logic? Miss Sam didn't say anything wrong. This thing is very thin, very light\u2014thin because the groove is shallow; light because if it were too heavy, it would have been discovered by centuries of experts\u2014hidden in the back cover of a counterfeit book made by William Jagger. Let's say it's a piece of paper?\" [904]\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[905] (1) A well-known American lingerie brand. [906] Chapter 10\n[907] Shakespeare appears\n[908] There was nothing else to do in the British Museum. The sergeant was especially impatient and wanted to leave as soon as possible, so everyone said goodbye and left. [909] Gordon Rowe accompanied them to the main entrance and tapped the copper mustache of Shakespeare's head with his knuckle. \"The old man is really smiling! It's no wonder, Petty, after centuries, a human thing finally happened in a museum.\" [910] \"Trickery!\" Pecins said sternly. \"Let go of my hand! I have a jealous father with eyes in the back of his head... Goodbye, Gordon.\" [911]\"Ah, splendid,\" the young man said. \"When can I see you again?\" [912]\"I'll let you know,\" Petkins said primly, and turned to follow the sergeant and Rean. [913]Gordon grabbed her hand. \"Petty, let me see you now!\" [914]\"Now?\" [915]\"I'll go to your father's office to see you. You're going there, aren't you?\" [916] \"Yes...yes!\" [917] \"Can I come along?\" [918] \"Oh, you're a persistent young man!\" Percy said, her face flushing for the twelfth time, hating herself for it. \"Well\u2014if your father lets you go.\" [919] \"Oh, he'll let me go,\" Rowe said cheerfully, slamming the back door shut. He took Percy's hand and they walked briskly across the sidewalk to meet the others. Dromio, the red-haired driver of the sleek black Lincoln limousine, stood smiling by the curb. [920] \"Inspector,\" the young man said eagerly, \"do you mind if I come along? Excellent, I can tell from your eyes that you don't mind.\" [921] Sam looked at him sternly: \"You \u2013\"\n[922] Mr. Philo Rene chuckled. \"Well, Inspector, I think it's a splendid idea. I'll drive you down to the downtown area. My car is right here, and I need a break from all these worries. It's time for a strategic meeting, and Gordon is a smart young man. Inspector, we should discuss this, or are you too busy to bother with us?\" [923] Roe said, \"There's a friend here.\" [924] The inspector grumpily said, \"With the current