re making a storm in a teapot - overreacting. Someone stole something, returned it with interest. As far as we know, there's no real crime here, is there?\" [935] \"Just a curious question, is there nothing more important?\" [936] She shrugged. \"I'm sorry, my mind isn't sharp today. I can't think of anything else.\" [937] \"No crime, hmm?\" The inspector said sharply. [938] \"Ah!\" Reine smiled faintly. \"Inspector, do you think there's a crime?\" [939] \"Of course! What happened to poor old Donohue?\" [940] The old gentleman closed his eyes for a moment. \"Missing guard. Honestly, I agree it looks suspiciously violent, but that's for the police. No, there's something else.\" [941] The tall young man sat by the door, his tired eyes sweeping from one person to another. Percy frowned. There was a silence, and then Sam shrugged and reached for the phone. \"Whether it's the police's business or not, I'm only really interested in this. I promised to find this old man, so I'll do my best. \" He called the head of the missing persons unit, Garrison, and then had a brief chat with Detective Inspector Grogan. \"There's no new development on Donohue. It seems he was abducted. I gave Grogan the serial number of the hundred-dollar bill in the book he returned, maybe he can find something.\" [942] \"Maybe.\" Laine agreed. \"Percy, I see you frowning again. Do you have something else in mind?\" [943] \"I'm trying to think.\" She said angrily. [944] \"The paper on the back cover.\" Mr. Rowe said briefly. [945] \"Oh, Mr. Rowe, of course!\" Percy exclaimed, blushing, \"The thing taken from the 1599 Jaggard back cover by the Blue Caps!\" [946] The old gentleman chuckled. \"You young people seem to think alike. Good, isn't it, Constable? \u2014 Well, don't frown. I told you Gordon was a good boy. Percy, that's what I mean. Look, thinking about the thin and light thing in the secret pocket of the book cover, the thief's unconventional behavior can be explained. About six weeks ago, someone broke into the Saxon Library and stole a book that was thought to be a 1599 Jagger. It's not hard to imagine that the theft was done by the same person \u2014 our witty Blue Cap. But the book was a fake and was returned. So the Blue Cap was looking for the real version! Well, how many genuine copies of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' are there in the world? Three. The Saxon was the third and the last to be found. He may have investigated the other two. After stealing the Saxon version, he found it was a fake, so he knew that the real Saxon version still existed. Later, Saxon donated the book to the British Museum, including the genuine Jagger. The thief managed to get into the museum and stole the third genuine Jagger, leaving another more valuable book. Two days later, he returned the Jagger. Percy, can you draw any conclusions from these events?\" [947] \"I see.\" Percy sucked on her lower lip. \"It's much clearer now. In fact, he returned the genuine Jagger to the museum, but he took something from the secret pocket, which shows that he's not interested in the 1599 Jagger itself, but in the thin and light thing inside. After taking it, he no longer needed the book, so he returned it like a gentleman.\" [948] \"Excellent!\" Rine exclaimed. \"Dear, your reasoning is superb.\" [949] \"Very clever.\" Rowe murmured softly. [950] \"And?\" The old gentleman asked. [951] \"Hmm,\" Percy blushed slightly. \"This leads to an odd thing - the 1599 Jagger is valuable, and if he were an ordinary thief, he would have left the book regardless of whether he wanted the thing hidden inside or not. But in the end, he left a hundred-dollar bill to cover the cost of repairing the book cover; even stranger, he initially left a book of great value in place of the one he stole - obviously because it looked very similar to the 1599 edition, or to show his honesty. Mr. Raine, these things show that he is an honest person, forced to commit an act of dishonesty, so he made compensation beforehand.\" [952] The elderly gentleman leaned forward, his eyes shining. After Percy finished speaking, he leaned back and pointed his long fingers at the inspector: \"Well. Old grump, what do you think? \" [953] The inspector coughed: \"Very good, I say very good!\" [954] \"Well, Inspector, your praise is insincere. Dear, it's perfect! You really lift our spirits. Yes, that's right.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHello, Inspector. Dr. What's the trouble?\" [745] Dr. Jott waved his blue-bound book without speaking. [746] Roe's smile instantly vanished. \"Not...?\" he asked, looking around at everyone's serious expressions. He took the book from the librarian's hand and slowly opened it, his face filled with surprise. He looked around again, puzzled. \"This isn't... This is the 1606 Jagger!\" he exclaimed loudly. \"I thought there were none left...\"\n[747] \"Obviously there are,\" the old gentleman said calmly. \"Gordon, if word of this gets out, there will be shrieks in the streets.\" [748] \"I know,\" Roe murmured. \"But, good heavens, where did this come from? Not from London, Dr. Sedgwick.\" [749] \"No,\" the Englishman replied slowly. [750] \"You won't believe it,\" Dr. Jott said with a shrug. \"We really had a thief here on Monday. Roe, someone left this book in the cabinet with the Jagger and took the 1599 one.\" [751] \"Oh...\" The young man said, \"I... I... I can't believe it! This is fantastic! You'll have to tell Lilydia and Crabber... Oh, this is too much! I'm sorry, but it's just too funny! A rare book stolen and then replaced with an even rarer one! It's Mrs. Saxon's lucky day! Madness, pure madness!\" [752] \"I think...\" The librarian nervously stroked his beard, \"You should probably ask Mrs. Saxon to come here right away, Rowe. After all...\"\n[753] \"Of course.\" The young man gently stroked the 1606 Jagger, then handed it back to Dr. Jotter, giving Mrs. Pecunia's arm a light squeeze, and left the room with a triumphant smile. [754] \"What a flippant young man,\" Dr. Sedgwick said disapprovingly. \"I'm afraid I can't appreciate his light-heartedness. We can't accept this... we can't just take it at face value, Dr. Jotter. We need to conduct a more thorough examination. It may be difficult to determine its authenticity.\" [755] Dr. Jotter's eyes glinted like a hunter's. \"Yes, yes.\" He rubbed his hands together, pleased that the stolen book was still in the thief's possession, hoping the thief wouldn't return the book and demand the one-of-a-kind copy left in the cabinet, \"I suggest we get to work right away. Sedgwick, we need to proceed with caution and not let any word slip out! We can ask Caspari from the Metropolitan Museum to keep it absolutely confidential...\"\n[756] Dr. Sedlar's face paled, and he stared at the ransacked cabinet as if hypnotized. [757] \"Or Professor Klaunenhielde of the Folkgeist,\u201d he mumbled. [758] Pinch sighed. \"We seem to have assumed that the 1599 Jager was stolen by the Blue Cap, but there's no evidence of that! Why couldn't the thief have been the second stranger on the bus or one of the seventeen teachers?\u201d\n[759] The sergeant shrugged, clearly finding the whole thing too absurd. [760] \"I don't think so, Pinch,\u201d said Lane softly. \"There were nineteen people on the bus, and all of them went into the museum. Eighteen returned to the bus station, the eighteenth being the second mysterious stranger you mentioned. In other words, our friend the Blue Cap disappeared from the museum, as did Donohue. The connection is too strong to be a coincidence. I believe it's highly likely that the Blue Cap stole the 1599 Jager, left this one here, and Donohue disappeared because he was following him.\u201d\n[761] \"Well, well,\" the curator said briskly. \"I believe time can explain everything. Now, Dr. Sidd