ds rather sad,\" Inspector Halloran said disapprovingly. [776] \"Oh, is it?\" the Englishman replied slowly, \"I assure you it is. I was looking forward to... Mr. Weiss told me about Saxton's donations...\"\n[777] \"Did he mention that the 1599 Jaggard was among them?\" Raine whispered. [778] \"Yes!\" Sedgwick sighed, bent over the cabinet, and put his monocle back on, \"It's beautiful, beautiful. I can't wait... What's this?\" His thin lips parted in excitement as he picked up the third book from the cabinet, examining the blank pages at the front and back. [779] \"What now?\" Raine stood up and walked quickly to the cabinet. [780] Dr. Sedgwick sighed heavily. \"I thought for a moment - I was wrong. Saxton bought this 'Henry IV' a few years before, and I studied it in London. The date is 1608. Jaggard deliberately advanced the date; he printed this book for stationer Thomas Pavel; it was probably printed in 1619. But I remember the cover was a deeper scarlet; obviously, under Saxton's careful management, it has faded a bit.\" [781] \"So that's it.\" The old gentleman said, \"You startled me, Doctor. What about 'Sir John Oldcastle'?\" [782] The future librarian lovingly stroked the first book in the cabinet and said solemnly, \"It's quite good. I saw this book at Sotheby's auction in 1913... The cover was very nice then, and it hasn't changed color at all, still the same golden brown! I'm not blaming Saxon for maliciously appropriating, please understand...\u201d\n[783] Dr. Jot quickly returned. \"I'm afraid I was wrong,\" he said enthusiastically, \"I couldn't find the stolen Jagger. Of course, we'll continue to search.\" [784] Mrs. Lady Saxon stormed into the room, like an angry elephant, and no one could stop her. She was large and imposing, with wild green eyes that glowed like a beast's, and she terrified the scholars, the librarian, and the entire unhappy group of donors. She was followed by Gordon Rowe, who was smiling, and an old man in a dusty tailcoat who 