y. [796] \"Dr. Sedra, our new head librarian,\" interjected the young Rowe. \"Come on, Keller, don't be rude! Dr. Sedra, this is Mr. Keller, the librarian of the Saxen Library.\" [797] \"Sedra, huh?\" Keller grumbled. \"Sedra, huh? Well, well.\u201d He tilted his thin head and grinned mischievously at the British man. Dr. Sedra looked back, feeling slighted yet puzzled, and then shrugged. [798] \"Mrs. Saxen, could I explain?\" he flashed a charming smile and stepped forward. \"This is the most\u2026\" They moved aside, and Dr. Sedra continued to whisper rapidly. Mrs. Saxen seemed like a judge who had already convicted the criminal, her expression indifferent yet hostile. [799] Philo Vance quietly returned to his chair in the corner of the room, closing his eyes and stretching out his long legs. Percy sighed and turned to look at Gordon Rowe, who pulled her aside and spoke to her animatedly in her ear. [800] Keller and Dr. Jott engaged in a calm and sincere discussion about the quiet 1606 edition of Jagger lying on the table. Sam the policeman wandered aimlessly, occasionally groaning at the librarian's words. [801] \"The words printed on the title page\u2026\"\n[802] \"Harvey-Phillips said\u2026\"\n[803] \"\u2026including the stolen sonnet\u2026\"\n[804] \"But is it an octavo or a quarto?\" [805] \"So about the Bodrian version\u2026\"\n[806] \"\u2026does indeed show that Jagger stole two poems from Haywood's 'The Triumph of Love' and placed them in his 1612 collection\u2026\"\n[807] \"The form was exactly\u2026\"\n[808] \"Until 1608, Jagger was just a publisher. It was only after he bought James Robert's printing shop in Barbican, probably in 1606\u2026\"\n[809] The sergeant roared again, plunging into a fit of rage, pacing around the room. [810] Dr. Jocelyn and the grim-faced Clerk looked up, momentarily ceasing their argument. [811] \"Ladies and gentlemen,\" the librarian raised his voice, stroking his beard, \"Mr. Clarke and I have agreed that this 1606 edition of Jagger's work is genuine!\" [812] \"Listen to this,\" the sergeant said, sounding glum. [813] \"Are you sure?\" Dr. Sedley turned his head from Mrs. Saxon. [814] \"I don't care!\" Mrs. Saxon shrieked. \"I still think it's a disgraceful way to repay Mr. Saxon's generosity\u2014\"\n[815] \"I told you, she's a real pain in the neck,\" the young Rowe said with a clear accent. [816] \"Shut up, you impulsive idiot!\" Percy hissed under his breath. \"The snake-woman will hear you!\" [817] \"Let her hear it,\" the young man smiled. \"She's a big, blustering whale.\" [818] \"I really don't think it's a fake,\" Jules Renet spoke up from the corner. [819] The doorkeeper with the garlic nose entered the room and walked over to Dr. Jott. [820] \"What's this, Bertram?\" the curator said indifferently. \"I think we can wait\u2014\"\n[821] \"I don't care,\" Bergh said expressionlessly, turning to leave. [822] \"Wait,\" Philo Vance said. He had stood up, his eyes fixed on the bag in Bergh's hand, a flash of intelligence lighting up his clear features. \"Dr. Jotter, if I were you, I'd check that bag. With this madness going on, anything could happen\u2026\"\n[823] They all looked at his face and the doorman's hand in confusion. [824] \"You think\u2014\" Dr. Jotter licked his whiskery lips. \"Very well, Bergh, give it to us.\" [825] Dr. Sedra and Crabbe stood like loyal guards on either side of the curator. [826] It was a neat, well-wrapped package, wrapped in ordinary brown paper and tied with a cheap red string. A label was pasted on the wrapping paper, bearing Dr. Jotter's name and the museum's address. The writing was in blue ink, and the small print was very clear. [827] \"Burch, who sent this?\" Dr. Jotter asked slowly. [828] \"A young, arrogant messenger,\" Burch said with a bad temper. [829] \"Oh.\" Dr. Jotter began to untie the string. [830] \"I'll do it, you idiot!\" The inspector suddenly roared, lunging forward and grabbing the package with haste, but very cautiously, \"