ocused intently on checking it. Perkins leaned close to the young Rowe, and as he saw Dr. Sedgwick's expression while watching Klauber, he almost exclaimed in surprise. The man had always worn a polite mask, but now the mask had fallen away, and his face displayed a strange, almost disappointed anger; his expression was quite savage, and the cold glasses over his right eye only accentuated it. For a moment, the mask slipped back on, and he carefully displayed his interest again. Perkins turned his head, looking into Rowe's eyes, and he caught that incredible expression as well. So, the two of them fixed their gaze on Dr. Sedgwick. [847] \"This is the Saxon J\u00e4ger,\" Klauber exclaimed sharply. [848] \"Oh, I'm such an idiot!\" Sam Inspector suddenly exclaimed, startling everyone. He rushed out of the Saxon room and hurried down the corridor. [849] \"Miss Sedgwick, your father\u2014\" Dr. Sedgwick smirked, \"seems to be a very impatient gentleman.\" [850] \"Dr. Sedgwick, my father\u2014\" Perkins countered, \"sometimes he's a very astute gentleman. He's thinking of practical things. I believe he's gone to chase the messenger, and we didn't think of doing that.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWe're dealing with an honest, even conscientious thief - something that has never been seen in the history of theft. The true Villon (1)! And more?\" [955] \"I think things are clear,\" the young man suddenly said. \"He returned the original fake Jagger without cutting open the cover, which means he is very familiar with rare books. I can tell you, I've seen the fake book, and it's not bad at all, something that most people wouldn't notice. He checked the book and immediately found that it was not real. Since he was only looking for the genuine 1599 Jagger, he didn't even touch the fake one and returned it