 the first edition of 'The Devout Pilgrim' from 1599. It may provide some precise dates. Jagger was involved in the distribution of many publications in Elizabethan London, and his name is closely associated with the literary world. Good, good, this is certainly a logical step. Gordon, what do you think?\" [970] \"I'll look into it too,\" Rowe said quietly. [971] \"Excellent. Inspector, will you continue to pursue Donohue?\" [972] \"As much as I can. I'll have Detective Sergeant Grayson do most of the work.\" [973] \"Indeed, that's his job. Inspector, I don't see any financial gain in this case.\" [974] \"You're right,\" the inspector said loudly, \"but it annoyed me, so I'll indulge myself.\" [975] \"Still as stubborn as ever,\" the old man chuckled. \"I have a suggestion for you, if you're investigating this case out of curiosity. Why not investigate Dr. Hamnett Sedra?\" [976] The sergeant was startled. Pechins stopped lighting a cigarette for Rowe. \"That duke? Why?\" [977] \"Let's say it's intuition,\" Rene murmured. \"Did you notice the strange look our friend Klaber gave Dr. Siddartha?\" [978] \"Good heavens, yes!\" exclaimed Percy, \"Gordon, you noticed it too.\" [979] \"You call him Gordon?\" the officer muttered suspiciously. [980] \"It was purely accidental.\" Mr. Lawrie hastened to say, \"Miss Sam was too excited. Miss Sam, please call me Mr. Lawrie... Yes, Patty, I noticed that, I've been trying to make sense of it all.\" [981] \"What's all this about Gordon, Petty?\" the sergeant growled. \"So familiar.\" [982] \"All right, all right, sergeant,\" J. R. Rine interceded. \"Don't let your personal feelings get in the way of the discussion. You know you're an old fossil. Young people aren't like they used to be.\" [983] \"Dad,\" Peasmarsh blushed. [984] \"Not like your time,\" Roarke added. [985] \"Introductions, flirting, kissing in dark corners,\" Rine continued with a smile. \"Well, sergeant, you have to accept these things. I said Clurman was a slippery