Dr. Sedra's tie, Reine?\" Reine continued to ask, his expression unchanged. [2478] The British man swayed, his eyes pained. \"Brown.\" - It was actually sapphire blue. [2479] \"Thank you.\" Reine returned the handkerchief and briefcase to Perkins. \"Inspector, this man is not Dr. Hamnet Sedra, he is William Sedra, sometimes called Dr. Alessi.\" [2480] The British man suddenly collapsed into a chair, burying his face in his hands. [2481] \"God, how did you know?\" Sam whispered. [2482] Raine sighed. \"Inspector, this is basic work. On May 6th, Dr. Alessi, or William Sedra, went to your office and entrusted you with that envelope. That person could not be Dr. Hamnett Sedra, as he himself said. Dr. Hamnett Sedra was in London on May 7th for a reception in his honor. The man with the envelope, of course, was the one who wrote the symbols inside - he admitted as much in your office that morning. What does the paper and symbols represent?\" [2483] \"Ah, just... damn, I don't know.\" The inspector said. [2484] Raine tiredly said: \"The paper was a light gray, with the heading 'Saxony Library' in a deeper gray. This and the form of the symbols immediately reminded me.\" [2485] \"What does it mean? We just made a mistake. You were lucky, you just guessed right.\" [2486] \"That's right. In other words, William Sedra wrote Wm SHe upside down on the paper. To correctly read those symbols, one must turn the paper upside down, which is very unusual. Someone picks up a paper with a heading, intending to write on it, and instinctively puts the paper right side up \u2013 that is, with the name and address at the top. However, the person who wrote these symbols took the paper and wrote it upside down! Why?\" [2487] \"I see.\" Percy sighed. \"Unless it was an accident, he couldn't see the printed markings!\" [2488] \"Yes, dear, that's exactly it. It seems impossible, but it's more likely that Dr. Aless accidentally turned the paper over and wrote a few letters, not realizing it would make any difference to the person who saw the symbols later. However, another possibility is logically possible, and I cannot ignore it. I asked myself: if it were true, what kind of miracle could this phenomenon create? Why didn't Dr. Aless see the deep gray print on the Saxon paper? Was he blind? But that's hard to believe. The person who came to your office seemed to have good eyesight. Then I remembered something else, and suddenly saw the answer... the mustache.\" [2489] The British man lifted his pain-filled eyes, now filled with curiosity. He murmured, \"Mustache?\" [2490] \"You know,\" Reine smiled, \"he still doesn't understand what's wrong with the fake mustache he's wearing! Mr. Sedra, the mustache you wore that day was terrifying! It was a shocking blue-green, and who knows what other colors!\" [2491] Sedgwick opened his mouth wide, groaning, \"Oh, I bought it at a costume shop. I guess I didn't explain it well enough. The shopkeeper thought I wanted a... a ridiculous mustache for some crazy thing like dressing up as a ghost...\u201d\n[2492] \"Unfortunately,\" Renault said coldly, \"but the mustache and the note match. I think the person who wrote the symbol is very likely to be completely colorblind. I've heard of such cases, so I asked Dr. Martini. He told me that complete colorblindness is extremely rare. But when it happens, such a person sees all colors as different shades of gray, like a pencil sketch. He also mentioned another possibility - that he may not be completely colorblind, but has no color perception. That's better than being colorblind, but he can't see the differences in brightness of printed colors. When Dr. Martini checked the Saxon library note, he was quite sure that the person who wrote the symbol was affected by this visual defect.\" [2493] The British man moved, growled, \"I've never seen a color.\" [2494] They were silent for a moment. Renault sighed and continued, \"So I believe in my heart that Dr. Alessi is colorblind. Sir, you were just tormented by the same situation. You guessed the colors of Miss Sam's handkerchief and Mr. Rowe's tie, not knowing the real colors. Now you call yourself Hamnett Sedgwick, but Hamnett Sedgwick is not colorblind! We first saw him in this museum's Saxon room. He checked the repaired cabinet - the one with the stolen 1599 Jagger - and correctly identified the colors of the book covers in it, as well as the different shades of the same color. He could distinguish that a book cover was golden brown, which a colorblind person could not distinguish such subtle differences. Since you are either William or Hamnett, and Hamnett's vision is normal, while William is colorblind - you are colorblind, obviously you are William, and this reasoning is very simple. I proposed this test to see if you were lying. You did lie. Most of the story you told us in the hospital was fabricated, although I suspect there were some truths. Now please tell the whole story to my friend.\" [2495] He sat down in a chair and wiped his lips again. [2496] \"Okay,\" the British man's voice was low, \"I am William Zedra.\" [2497] When he first visited the inspector, he was Dr. Aless. He gave the letter with the symbol to the inspector; in case something happened to him while he was searching for Shakespeare's documents, it could serve as a clue - at the time, he thought the likelihood of something happening was very small. On June 20th, he didn't call because he couldn't, the very small possibility had occurred. His brother Hamnett - William found out then - accepted the position of director of the British Museum, with the sole purpose of approaching the 1599 Jagger in the Saxon collection. William stole the Jagger from the museum the night he did, and then Hamnett kidnapped William, which happened shortly after Donohue appeared, on the same night Donohue was also kidnapped by Hamnett. The Irishman had been unconscious for a while, so his sense of time was distorted... from William stealing the Jagger to the police rescuing him from the abandoned house, he was a prisoner. [2498] No matter how Hamnett threatened him, William refused to reveal the location of the documents. Of course, Donohue didn't know anything, and couldn't tell Hamnett anything. Hamnett, in order to go to the museum to work and pretend to be innocent, came and went to the place of confinement in a hurry, and couldn't do it regularly, so he became desperate. One day, he told William that he knew the documents were in William's house, and he put a bomb in the cellar to blow up the house and the documents! The bomb was made by a black market chemist on his own. It was then that William realized that his brother's true intention was not to preserve the Shakespeare documents, but to destroy them! [2499] \"But why?\" Rowe raised his fist and roared, \"That's... that's the most barbaric act of destruction! Oh, why destroy it?\" [2500] \"Is your brother insane?\" Percy exclaimed. [2501] The British man clenched his lips, looking at Reine, who was calmly looking into the distance. \"I don't know,\" he said. [2502] Hamnet set the timer on the bomb for twenty-four hours later. William realized that if he let the bomb go off, the documents would be destroyed and forever lost. He had to give up resistance, knowing that any delay would be more advantageous. He might be able to untie himself and save the documents. So he told Hamnet the location and method of opening the secret cabinet, but he couldn't escape. Hamnet proudly told him that he would go back to William's house and destroy the documents himself, and that he had plenty of time. He would remove the bomb's safety pin\u2026 Hamnet took William's key\u2014the original one\u2014and William never saw him alive again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2140] \"There's someone in the hall who wants to see you. He called as soon as you left. He seemed very upset, Mr. Jules.\" [2141] \"Who is it?\" [2142] \"He said his name is Jot.\" [2143] They hurried towards the hall. The entire manor was designed to resemble a medieval British castle, and the layout of the hall echoed the past. Standing at a distance, with his hands behind his back, was the director of the British Museum, Dr. Jot, pacing under the tragic mask that Rowe had carefully placed at one end of the hall. [2144] The three of them rushed towards him. [2145] \"Dr. Jot,\" Rowe said slowly, \"I apologize for keeping you waiting. There were some unexpected events... Your face looks as grim as that mask. What's the matter?\" [2146] \"Unexpected events?\" Dr. Jotter said angrily. \"So you know about it?\" He nodded reluctantly at Percy and Rowe. [2147] \"The explosion?\" [2148] \"Explosion? What explosion? Oh, no! I mean Dr. Sedra.\" [2149] \"Dr. Sedra!\" The three of them exclaimed. [2150] \"He's missing.\" [2151] The librarian leans against an oak table, with bloodshot eyes. [2152] \"Missing?\" Percy furrows his brow, \"How could that be? We saw him on Saturday, didn't we, Gordon?\" [2153] \"Yes, indeed.\" The librarian rumbles, \"He came in on Saturday morning and stayed for a few minutes, seemed fine. Before he left, I asked him to call me at home on Sunday \u2013 last night \u2013 to discuss some museum matters. He agreed and left.\" [2154] \"He didn't call?\" Raine whispers. [2155] \"No. I contacted the Seneca Inn where he was staying, and he wasn't there. I waited for him all day today, hoping he'd get in touch, but nothing. Dr. Jotter shrugs, \"It's \u2013 it's stupid! He didn't say he was leaving. I thought maybe he was sick, so I called the inn again this afternoon. They said they haven't seen him since Saturday morning.\" [2156] \"That doesn't mean he disappeared Saturday!\" Rowe countered. [2157] \"I know. It's just strange. I don't know what to do. Should I call the police or\u2026 I tried to reach your father, Miss Samuels, but the office secretary said\u2026\"\n[2158] \"First Donoho, then Dr. Aless, and now Sedra,\" Percy lamented. \"These people are disappearing one by one! It's \u2013 it's unacceptable!\" [2159] \"Unless Sedra is Aless,\" Rowe said. [2160] Dr. Jotter ran his fingers through his hair. \"Oh, my God!\" [2161] Pinchot frowned. \"I don't think that necessarily means Dr. Aless is Sedra, or that he escaped with the file.\" [2162] \"Dear Miss Sampson, the hotel staff said all of his things were still in the room. I don't think a person running away would do that. What file are you talking about\u2014\"\n[2163] Raine looked exhausted, with deep circles under his eyes and skin that looked like crumpled parchment. He wearily shook his head: \"These conjectures won't get us anywhere. It's a surprising turn of events\u2026 The only thing I can suggest is to find out what happened to Sedra.\" [2164] Pinchot and Roebling arrived in town late at night. They parked their car outside the Seneca Hotel and went inside to find the manager. After some hesitation, they were granted permission to look at Dr. Sedra's room. The room seemed tidy, with British-cut clothes neatly hanging in the closet, freshly laundered sheets and pillowcases in the dresser, and three unopened suitcases and bags. The manager seemed reluctant to involve the police, glancing at Pinchot's credentials\u2014which, of course, were the detective's\u2014and reluctantly allowing the search of the room. [2165] The luggage and clothes were all British. There were some letters in the room, with postmarks from \"London\" and addressed to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, apparently from former British colleagues. The passport and visa were in order, issued to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, with a familiar-looking photo attached. [2166] \"It's Sedra, no doubt,\" Rowe said grimly. \"I'm starting to get paranoid about this. There's no sign here that this person wanted to flee the country.\" [2167] \"Ugh!\" Peachins muttered under his breath. \"Gordon, take me home, and\u2014and, kiss me!\" [2168] Chapter 25\n[2169] Murder\n[2170] The sun shone brightly. The fire had been extinguished, and the smoke had dispersed with the night, leaving only burnt-out ashes, piles of rubble, and charred trees to bear witness to the explosion of the night before. Firefighters and police were busy digging through the rubble. A dark-skinned man calmly directed the entire operation, his eyes sharp and eager to clear away the debris so he could descend into the cellar to see what was inside. [2171] They stood by the edge of the woods, the warm morning breeze rustling their clothes. Pauling watched the workers with a cold eye. [2172] \"See that guy with the hawk eyes? He's an explosives expert. I think since he's involved, he should do a good job! I want to see how this strange thing happened.\" [2173] \"You mean he'll find something in that pile of garbage?\" Roeve asked. [2174] \"That's why he's here.\" [2175] The workers made rapid progress, quickly clearing the debris blocking the cave. They shoveled the garbage to thirty feet away, hand over hand. Once the cellar was almost clear enough to descend, the silent man crawled into the hole and disappeared. Ten minutes later, he reappeared, looking around as if measuring the extent of the explosion, and then disappeared again, this time into the woods. He reappeared again and descended into the cellar. The third time he emerged, he wore a satisfied expression, holding in his hands a jumbled mess of iron pieces, rubber, glass, and wires. [2176] \"How's it going?\" Pauling asked. [2177] \"Sir, the evidence is here.\" The explosives expert said, picking up a fragment of a clock-like instrument, \"A time bomb.\" [2178] \"Ah!\" Mr. Philo Vance said. [2179] \"Primitive, homemade. Set to go off at six o'clock with yellow dynamite.\" [2180] Percy, Roe, and Vance all thought the same thing, but Vance was the first to ask: \"When was the bomb placed?\" [2181] \"At six o'clock on Sunday evening - if the bomb went off last night at six o'clock. It's a twenty-four-hour timer.\" [2182] \"Six o'clock on Sunday evening.\" Perkins repeated slowly, \"So it was put on before Maxwell was attacked on Sunday night!\" [2183] \"You're right, Perri.\" Rowe said, \"If the person who put the bomb knew there were documents in the house, his purpose was to destroy the documents, so he knew the stuff was in the house but didn't know exactly where. It's difficult -\"\n[2184] \"The epicenter of the explosion was the cellar.\" The expert spat on the charred rock. [2185] Reine let out another \"Ah.\" [2186] \"The second visitor \u2013 the one who took the documents out of the secret cabinet \u2013\" Perkins looked pointedly at Reen, \"could not have been the one who planted the bomb. It's obvious. The second visitor knew where the documents were; the bomber didn't, as you said, Gordon\u2026\" Perkins' words were interrupted by a coarse roar, coming from the workers digging through the cellar debris. They all turned around. [2187] \"What is it?\" Bowlin called, running over quickly. [2188] Three people were bending over something, their heads almost touching. One of them turned around, pale and trembling. He managed to speak: \"Sir, there's a body here. From the look on his face, he was \u2013 murdered.\" [2189] The young man ran towards the edge of the house foundation, stepping on black ash. Reen followed slowly, his face pale and anxious. [2190] Roarke looked at the scene in front of him and roughly pushed Perkins aside. \"Petty, get away from here,\" he said hoarsely. \"You'd better go stand under that tree over there. This \u2013 it's not good.\" [2191] \"Oh,\" Perkins said. Her nostrils flared, and she silently obeyed his request. [2192] The men all widened their eyes and focused intently on the deep hole. A young, red-faced policeman crawled out from the corner of the cellar, bent over, and began to shake and vomit... The body was badly burned, almost unrecognizable - one leg and one arm were missing, and the clothes were completely burnt. [2193] \"How do you know he was murdered?\" Reen asked sternly. [2194] An older policeman's mouth twisted: \"He's not completely burnt, I can see the hole.\" [2195] \"Hole?\" Rowe croaked. [2196] The man sighed. \"Three holes. In his stomach, clean and neat. Those are bullet holes, sir, don't forget.\" [2197] Three hours later, Raine, Detective Bolling, Peachins, and the young Roe sat silently in the office of the Prosecutor of Tarrytown. Someone had called to dispatch a car to take the body to the morgue in Tarrytown. Apart from the usual procedures of collecting the remains and their belongings, Bolling ordered no one to touch the body. The officers searched for fragments of the clothing, hoping to find buttons that could provide clues to the victim's identity. However, the explosion had damaged the body so much that the officer soon gave up searching. The explosives expert happily said that the body was not turned into atoms, a miracle. [2198] They sat at the prosecutor's desk, staring at the items on the table. This was the only item taken from under the body that could be used as a clue \u2013 a cheap British-made watch with a leather strap. If traced back to its source, it would be a waste of time. The watch case was almost gone, leaving only a small triangular piece of glass. The synthetic metal used to make the watch, despite its surface turning gray-black, was not damaged by the explosion. There was one strange thing: the hands pointed to 12:26; the face of the watch had a deep cut that went over the number \"10\" and extended to the edge of the watch. [2199] \"That's odd,\" the prosecutor, a young man with worried eyes, said. \"Bolling, didn't you tell me the body was found face down, with the arm wearing this watch pressed under the stomach?\" [2200] \"Yes.\" [2201] \"Then the cut on the case of the watch was not left by the explosion.\" [2202] \"There are other suspicious things,\" Percy said. \"If the watch stopped because of the explosion, the pointer should have stopped at six o'clock, but it didn't.\" [2203] The prosecutor looked at her approvingly. \"Right! I didn't even think of it myself. You're Sam Chief's daughter, aren't you?\" [2204] The coroner rushed in - a short, bald man with a pink face and a soft chin - \"Hello, hello! I just finished checking the mess inside the body.\" \"He was murdered, right?\" Rowe asked eagerly. [2205] \"Yes.\" [2206] \"Yes, indeed. Of course, it's difficult to make a definite diagnosis based on the condition of the body, but I estimate that he died more than thirty-six hours ago, so he must have died around midnight on Sunday.\" [2207] \"Midnight on Sunday!\" Percy glared at Roe, who glared back at her. Mr. Jury Ring shuddered. [2208] \"This is consistent with the time on the watch,\" the prosecutor concluded. \"Twelve o'clock - twenty - six minutes. The watch must have stopped at the time of the murder. He was killed at zero point twenty-six on Monday.\" [2209] The little bald man continued, \"He was shot from the front, at close range. Three bullets.\" He dropped three deformed bullets on the table. \"The slash on the case is strange. There is also a deep cut on the wrist. The beginning of the cut on the wrist is exactly where the slash on the case stops.\" [2210] \"In other words,\" Roe asked, \"you think the same attack caused the cuts on both the wrist and the case?\" [2211] \"That's the standard answer!\" [2212] \"This is our axe-wielder,\" Roe's eyes blazed with anger, \"or at least the person who used the axe... Doctor, could these cuts have been made by an axe?\" [2213] \"Yes, it couldn't have been a knife. The blade of the weapon must have been wide and had a handle.\" [2214] \"That settles it,\" Pauling said. \"Someone used an axe on this guy, hitting his wrist, smashing his watch, stopping it, and injuring his wrist. And, I think, the killer fed him lead pellets.\" [2215] \"There's one more thing.\" The coroner pulled a small key wrapped in a tissue from his pocket. \"Pauling, your men just brought it over, they found it in a fragment of a pants pocket near the body. Someone has identified it\u2014\"\n[2216] \"Maxwell?\" [2217] \"The caretaker of the house? Yes, Maxwell identified this as the original key to the front door.\" [2218] \"Original!\" The two young men exclaimed in unison. [2219] \"Strange.\" Pauling said. \"Wait a minute!\" He picked up the prosecutor's phone and dialed the headquarters in Tarrytown. He spoke briefly to someone and then hung up the phone, saying, \"Yes. My men told me this is Dr. Alessi's key. The key taken by the masked man who tied Maxwell up in the garage that night was a copy.\" [2220] \"The only original?\" Percy gasped. [2221] \"That's what Maxwell said.\" [2222] \"So, there's nothing to doubt,\" the prosecutor sighed contentedly. \"The body must be that of Dr. Allais.\" [2223] \"Really?\" Reine murmured. [2224] \"Don't you think so?\" [2225] \"Dear sir, a key does not necessarily represent an owner. However, I suppose that logically, it makes sense.\" [2226] \"Well, I'm busy.\" The forensic expert said, \"And there's one more thing. I think you need the deceased's physical features. He's five feet eleven inches tall, has light yellow or golden hair, weighs about one hundred fifty-five pounds, and is between forty-five and fifty-five years old. I couldn't find any other identifying marks.\" [2227] \"Sedra.\" Percy whispered. [2228] \"Bingo.\" Roe impatiently said, \"A British doctor named Sedra was involved in this case, and he disappeared from his New York hotel on Saturday. This description fits him perfectly.\" [2229] \"You don't mean that guy!\" Paul shouted. [2230] \"I do mean him. However, the identification seems a bit vague. This Sedra person is also suspected to be Dr. Alessi\u2014\"\n[2231] \"So that's it!\" Bauer exclaimed hopefully. \"Don't forget, we carried the body with Dr. Aless' key. If Sedra is Aless, then everything falls into place.\" [2232] \"I'm not so sure,\" Rowe stammered. \"There are actually two possibilities. We're arguing over here because we haven't analyzed it thoroughly enough. The first possibility is that Sedra and Aless are the same person, just like you said, Mr. Bauer. In this case, the body \u2013 the features match both of them \u2013 clears up the mystery of the two missing persons. But if Sedra and Aless are not the same person, then we can only conclude that they are 'physically similar.' We've been avoiding this conclusion because it seems \u2013 uh \u2013 like a clich\u00e9 from a novel, but you can't ignore it.\" [2233] Reen remained silent. [2234] \"Well,\" Bauer stood up, \"maybe these ramblings can solve your problem. I just want to know one thing: who is this corpse? Dr. Aless? Or that damn Britisher Sedra?\" [2235] Two important things happened on Tuesday morning. Chief Sam returned victorious from Ohio, having apprehended the jewel thief who was safely behind bars in an iron window; and the mystery of the \"physically similar\" was solved. [2236] Chapter 26\n[2237] Resurrection\n[2238] The next morning, the two old men and two young people sat under the large oak tree in the peaceful, serene garden of Reine. \"We're here again because...\" the officer cheerfully told Reine, \"Petty told me she and this young man have practically lived here! We have some interesting news to tell you.\" [2239] \"News?\" the old gentleman shrugged, looking tired and weary, then he smiled weakly, a little of his old-time pleasantness creeping into his voice, \"\u2018Fill me with your rich voice, and let all my senses be fed, / Till I am as a seagull come to cormorant's nest on the head.\u2019 (1) I believe the harvest is bountiful.\" [2240] The officer grinned, in a very good mood. \"Judge for yourself!\" he reached into his pocket and pulled out an envelope, \"This morning I received an unexpected telegram from the old man Truncus.\" [2241] The telegram read:\n[2242] Further investigation into Hamnet Sedley reveals an interesting fact. The previous telegram informed you that Hamnet's brother William was missing, and it has now been discovered that William and Hamnet are twin brothers. William has arrived in America, arriving in New York from Bordeaux in late March. He is wanted by the Bordeaux police for illegal entry and malicious destruction of the private library of a French book collector, with the intent to steal rare books. The Frenchman was brutally beaten after discovering that William had damaged the cover of a 1599 edition of Shakespeare's \"The Passionate Pilgrim.\" William's behavior is strange, and he is well-off, like Hamnet, a book collector, and disappeared from Britain three years ago under the pseudonym of Dr. Allis. He served as an expert in rare book auctions, purchasing books for millionaire collectors. His best customer was the recently deceased Sir Humphrey. Both William and Hamnet have no fingerprints on record, and they have no discernible features to consider, but they bear a striking resemblance to each other.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[411] They passed through the marble entrance hall and entered a small reception room filled with pungent paint odors. Furniture was stacked in the center of the room, covered with messy canvas sheets, which were used by the painters. Painters were riding on scaffolding, painting the walls and ceilings with wet brushes. A piece of cloth covered the bust of the great British literary figure, Shakespeare, on the niche. Far away from the room was an iron gate leading to the elevator. [412] Percy sniffed, \"Dr. Quot, I don't know if I like them being decorated like this. Isn't it more respectful to keep the statues of Shakespeare, Jonson (1), and Marlowe (2) in their original form?\" [413] \"Good idea,\" the curator said, \"I also oppose it. But we have a radical board of directors. We barely managed to convince them to abandon the idea of installing a series of modern paintings in the Shakespeare Hall!\" He smiled and glanced sideways at Detective Sam, \"Shall we go to my office? Right next to this, thankfully, the paintbrush hasn't touched it yet.\" [414] He led them through a pile of dirty canvas to a door in a recessed room. His name was elegantly written on the wooden door. He led them into a large and bright room. The ceiling was high, and the oak bookshelves on the walls were filled with books. A young man was sitting in an armchair, engrossed in reading, and only looked up when they entered. [415] \"Ah, Rowe,\" Dr. Quot said loudly, \"I apologize for disturbing you. I want you to meet Detective Raine's friend.\" [416] The young man quickly stood up, smiling warmly and slowly taking off his tortoiseshell-rimmed glasses. He was tall; without his glasses, his features were friendly; his brown eyes had a scholarly air, but his shoulder line revealed an athletic build. [417] \"Miss Sam, this is Gordon Rowe, the most diligent newcomer to Britain,\" Dr. Jotter introduced. \"This is Inspector Sam.\" [418] The young man's gaze never left Percy, and he shook hands with the inspector. \"Hello! Dr. Jotter, do you know what's best to look at when your eyes are tired? I'll tell you. Sam...uh, I'm sorry, I don't like that name, it's not suitable. Let me think...ah! Inspector, I seem to have heard of you.\" [419] \"Thank you,\" the inspector said coldly. \"Don't let us bother you, this person with what name do you call him. Dr. Jotter, perhaps we should go elsewhere, let this young man look at his cheap novels.\" [420] \"Dad!\" Percy exclaimed. \"Oh, Mr. Rowe, don't mind my dad. You know, he might not like you making fun of the name 'Sam'. \" Her face grew redder, and the young man ignored the inspector's glare at him and continued to look at Percy with admiration. [421] \"Mr. Rowe, what kind of name would you give me?\" she asked. [422] \"Darling (3),\" Rowe said gently. [423] \"Percy Darling?\" [424] \"Oh\u2014just Darling.\" [425] \"Hey\u2014\" the inspector began to get angry. [426] \"Please sit down,\" Dr. Jotter's face was full of gentle smiles. \"Rowe, for the love of God, stop messing around. Miss Sam, please sit down.\" [427] Percy felt the young man's gaze somehow gently disturb her heart, and the blood in her veins suddenly boiled with some awareness. She sat down, and the inspector and Dr. Jotter sat down, while Mr. Rowe remained standing and gazed at her. [428] \"It's been a long wait,\" Dr. Jotter suddenly said. \"They just started. I mean, the painters. They haven't even touched the upstairs yet.\" [429] \"Oh,\" Sam inspector cleared his throat. \"I want to tell you\u2014\"\n[430] Gordon Rowe sat down, with a hint of a smile on his face. \"I seem to be out of place here,\" he said cheerfully. [431] Chief Inspector Sam wore a satisfied expression, but Perkins gave her father a charming glance and addressed the librarian, \"Dr. Jotter, you mentioned that you're included in the reorganization, right? ... Mr. Rowe, please stay.\" [432] Mr. Jotter leaned back in his swivel chair behind the large desk and looked around the room, sighing. \"That's right. It hasn't been officially announced, but I'm leaving, retiring. I've spent fifteen years of my life in this building, and I think it's time to take care of myself. I'm very clear about what I want to do. I've found a British-style house in northern Connecticut that I want to buy, and I plan to spend my retirement as a retired scholar, buried in books...\"\n[433] \"That's a good idea.\" The Chief Inspector said. \"But I wanted to say \u2013\"\n[434] \"It's so charming.\" Rowe whispered, still gazing at Perkins. [435] \"I heard Mr. Raine talk about you, and you really should rest.\" Perkins quickly said to the doctor. \"Doctor, when are you leaving?\" [436] \"I haven't decided yet. You know, we're in the process of hiring a new librarian. He's actually due to arrive tonight on a ship from England and will be here in the morning. We'll see how it goes. He'll need some time to get used to everything, of course, and I'll stay until he can handle things on his own.\" [437] \"Is this a social call, Miss Darling?\" the young man suddenly asked. [438] \"I've always thought Americans were reluctant to borrow paintings or books from Britain,\" Percy said, puzzled. \"Dr. Jute, I suppose this future librarian must be a very special book collector. Is he really an important figure?\" [439] The officer sitting in the chair fidgeted. [440] \"He's made a name for himself abroad,\" Dr. Jute's hand waved elegantly. \"But he's not a first-rate figure. He's been the director of a small museum in London for many years \u2013 the Kensington Museum. His name is Sedra, Hamnett Sedra...\"\n[441] \"Well, here comes a genuine Britisher,\" the young man said enthusiastically. [442] \"He was hired by our board chairman, James Wight.\" [443] Persimmon felt a little uncomfortable as she couldn't see the young man's admiring gaze. She raised her fine eyebrows. [444] \"Wight is a god among the wealthy, a cold man with an infinite passion for knowledge. Of course, Sedgwick was also warmly recommended by Sir John Humprey. Sir John has been one of Britain's most outstanding collectors of Elizabethan treasures for decades.\" Dr. Quill continued kindly. \"Sir John's recommendation carries weight. Sam Sergeant, you probably know about this, don't you?\" [445] The sergeant was about to speak, but cleared his throat: \"Of course, of course. But what we thought was\u2026\"\n[446] \"Don't you mind if I stay here?\" Mr. Rowe suddenly asked, \"I've always hoped someone would come.\" He smiled and closed the thick book he had just read, \"Today is my lucky day.\" [447] \"Not at all!\" Pechin said softly, her cheeks flushed, \"Oh - Dr. Jotter, I spent a long time in Britain when I was young -\"\n[448] \"Lucky Britain.\" The young man said seriously. [449] \"I feel that the most cultured British people think we are not only strange but also somewhat belligerent savages. I think the conditions provided for Sedra must be very good -\"\n[450] Mr. Jotter coughed lightly. \"Miss Sam, you're wrong. According to the financial situation of Britain, we can't pay Sedra the salary he would get in London, but he seems to be very eager to join us, so he immediately agreed to Mr. Wetherspoon's request. I think we're all the same - impractical.\" [451] \"That's true,\" the young man sighed, \"if I were more practical\u2014\"\n[452] \"It's strange,\" Miss Percy smiled, \"it doesn't sound like a normal British mindset.\" [453] The chief constable coughed loudly. \"Well, Petty. Mr. Jott is a busy man, and we don't want to waste his entire day discussing matters that don't concern us.\" [454] \"Oh, right, Chief Constable\u2014\"\n[455] \"I believe a man like Mr. Jott,\" Mr. Rowe said enthusiastically, \"would be more than happy to converse with a beautiful young lady like your daughter, Chief Constable\u2014\"\n[456] Sam's eyes flashed with urgency, and he ignored what the young man said: \"Dr. Jotter, we came here to ask about Donohue.\" [457] \"Donohue?\" The curator looked confused, looking at Mr. Rowe with his body leaning forward and his eyes shining, \"What about Donohue?\" [458] \"What about Donohue?\" the sergeant shouted, \"Oh, Donohue disappeared, that's what happened.\" [459] The young man's smile faded, and he whispered softly, \"Disappeared?\" [460] Dr. Jotter frowned. \"Are you sure, Sergeant? I guess you mean our special guards?\" [461] \"Of course! Don't you know he didn't come to work this morning?\" [462] \"Yes, I know! But I don't think it's a big deal.\" The librarian stood up and began to pace back and forth on the carpet behind the desk, \"Our doorman, Bert, did mention to me that Donohue didn't come to work this morning, but I didn't think it was\u2014oh, Rowe, you remember I mentioned it to you. We really like having him here, giving him a lot of freedom, he wouldn't have it anywhere else. Now the museum is closed\u2026 What happened? Chief, what's going on?\" [463] \"As far as we can tell,\" the chief of police answered laboriously, \"he left yesterday afternoon during the tour of the teachers, and no one has seen him since. He didn't go back to his apartment; he didn't even show up for his appointment with friends last night\u2014he just disappeared mysteriously.\" [464] \"That's strange, isn't it, Dr. Percy?\" Rowe said softly. [465] Gordon Rowe quietly put down the book. [466] \"Very strange, very strange,\" Dr. Jotter seemed quite uneasy, \"a group of teachers...Sergeant, they don't seem like the type to hurt anyone.\" [467] \"If you've been a police officer for as long as I have,\" the sergeant countered, \"you learn not to judge a book by its cover. I know you were the one who led them on the museum tour.\" [468] \"Yes.\" [469] \"Do you remember how many there were?\" [470] \"What? I don't know. Sergeant, I don't think I counted them.\" [471] \"Dr. Percy, did you happen to notice a middle-aged man with a thick, gray handlebar mustache and a blue hat?\" [472] \"Miss Sams, I'm usually so distracted, half the time I don't even pay attention to my surroundings.\" [473] \"I noticed that,\" Roeve lifted his thin chin, \"but only for a moment, not closely.\" [474] \"What a shame,\" the officer sneered, \"so Dr. Percy, you just led them around, huh?\" [475] \"That was my mistake,\" the curator shrugged, \"Miss Sams, why are you so eager to find this man in the blue hat?\" [476] \"Dr. Pechin, the man with the blue hat doesn't belong to this group of teachers,\" said Peachin. \"And we have very good reasons to believe that Donohue's disappearance is related to him.\" [477] \"Strange,\" muttered the young Rowe. \"Strange. Dr. Pechin, a museum secret! It sounds like something Donohue would do, his Irish temperament is simply romantic and beyond help.\" [478] \"You mean he noticed something odd about the man in the blue hat,\" thought Dr. Jotter. \"So he couldn't resist investigating on his own? Of course, it's very possible. I have confidence in Donohue's ability to take care of himself.\" [479] \"So where is he?\" asked the inspector skeptically. [480] Dr. Jotter shrugged again, obviously thinking the whole thing was trivial. He smiled and stood up. [481] \"Well, now that your business is done, Inspector, would you like to take a tour? Miss Sam, what about you? I know you've been to Britain, but we've recently received some very important donations that I think you'll be interested in. They're in a room we've named after Samuel Saxon. You know, he passed away not long ago \u2013\"\n[482] \"This \u2013\" the Inspector grimaced. [483] \"We'll love it,\" Percy interjected. [484] Dr. Jotter led the way like Moses, walking down the corridor past rows of canvas covered in paint, to a large reading room. The walls of the reading room were lined with bookshelves, which were also hung with canvas. Inspector Sam trudged wearily beside him, followed by Percy and the tall young man \u2013 an arrangement that added a new flush of red to Percy's cheeks. [485] \"Darling, don't mind me hovering beside you?\" the young man whispered. [486] \"I never shun the company of handsome men,\" Percy said uncomfortably. \"Mr. Rowe, though, I don't think I'll let you forget who you are. Have you been told that you're an incredibly rude young man?\" [487] \"My brother,\" Rowe said sternly. \"Once I blackened his eye. Darling, I don't know when I've met a girl like you\u2014\"\n[488] Dr. Jowett led them to the door at the other end of the reading room, saying loudly, \"Miss Sam, honestly, Mr. Rowe is probably more qualified to use the Saxon Room than I am. He's one of those prodigious child geniuses you've heard about.\" [489] \"Horrible,\" Percy shook her head, saying. [490] \"Don't believe a word of it,\" Rowe said immediately. \"Jowett, what you mean is\u2014\"\n[491] \"Oh, now you're calling me Miss Sam again?\" [492] He blushed. \"I apologize. Sometimes I just slip up. What Dr. Quirt meant was that it's a stroke of luck for me to be able to attract Samuel Saxon's attention. He left a lot of rare books to Britain in his will; he died a few months ago. As his prot\u00e9g\u00e9, my work here has a bit of an official nature, responsible for overseeing these books in their new home.\" [493] \"More and more terrifying, Mr. Rowe. I usually only take an interest in brainless young men with no obvious backing.\" [494] \"Now you're being deliberately cruel,\" he murmured, his eyes flickering. \"I swear to you, I'm qualified! In fact, I'm doing some innovative Shakespearean research. Mr. Saxon especially took care of me, putting me under his wing so I could continue my research here. Now that he's passed away, many of the Shakespearean materials are bequeathed to Britain in his will.\" [495] They entered a long, narrow room. From the fresh appearance, smell of turpentine, and lack of canvas coverings, it seemed to have just been renovated. There could have been thousands of books inside, most of which were open for browsing. A small portion was housed in wooden cabinets with glass covers \u2013 obviously more valuable books. [496] \"Just renovated,\" Dr. Jotter said. \"There are some truly unique things here, aren't there, Rowe? Of course, the books in this room haven't been displayed yet; these acquisitions arrived just a few weeks ago, after we closed.\" [497] \"This is -\" Dr. Jotter spoke in a tone of a cultural guide, slowly walking up to the nearest cabinet - \"a -\"\n[498] \"Hey!\" the constable exclaimed sharply, \"What's wrong with that cabinet over there?\" [499] Dr. Jotter and Rowe rushed over like startled birds, and Percy felt his breath quicken. [500] The constable pointed to a