ergeant said sternly, \"Alright, Dromio. The rest of you stay here. Joe, when I go to scout ahead, don't get any bright ideas. If you cooperate, I promise you won't suffer.\" [1738] He quickly climbed out of the car, crossed the open ground, stepped onto the porch, and despite his bulky frame, his movements were surprisingly light. The paint on the door was just as weathered as the wall paint, but it was still a sturdy piece of wood. There was a small bell next to the door. He didn't ring the bell, but tried to look through the windows, only to find that the tightly closed shutters didn't allow him to peek inside. He lightly descended the steps and disappeared to the left of the house. Three minutes later, he reappeared from the right, shaking his head. [1739] \"Damn it, it seems like an abandoned house. Alright, let's take a look!\" He strode confidently onto the porch and pressed the doorbell with force. [1740] For a moment - so quickly that it seemed he must have been peering through a crack - a man opened the door and stepped out. As soon as the door opened, a bell rang above it. The old-fashioned device hung above the door, and as soon as the door moved slightly, the spring attached to the bell would vibrate and jump. The man was tall and old, with a pale face covered in wrinkles. His cloudy gray eyes briefly rested on the sergeant, then looked at the sunlit carriage, and then back at him. [1741] \"Sir, is there something you need?\" His voice was shrill, \"Can I be of service?\" [1742] \"Is this the house of Dr. Aleis?\" [1743] The old man eagerly nodded, looking somewhat pleased. He smiled and scratched his head. \"Oh, yes, sir. Do you have news of him? I've been worried \u2013\"\n[1744] \"Ah, I see. Wait here,\" the sergeant walked to the edge of the porch, \"you all better come up. It seems we'll be waiting for a while.\" [1745] The emaciated old man led them through the narrow corridor to a small living room. The interior was cool. The furniture in the room was old and shiny, with an old carpet and old paintings. A pungent odor reminiscent of a funeral home filled the air. The old man busily opened the shutters and curtains, and the room seemed even more shabby and repulsive under the sunlight. [1746] The sergeant coldly said, \"First, we want to know who you are?\" [1747] The old man cheerfully smiled, \"Sir, my name is Maxwell. I take care of the house for Dr. Alley, doing all sorts of chores \u2014 cooking, cleaning, chopping wood, buying things in Tarrytown.\" [1748] \"Just a housekeeper? You're the only one here?\" [1749] \"Yes, sir.\" [1750] \"You said Dr. Alley isn't home?\" [1751] Maxwell's smile turned to a look of caution. \"I thought - you didn't know, did you? I thought you would have news of him, sir.\" [1752] Percy sighed, \"Damn it! Mr. Lane, you're right, he must have had an accident.\" [1753] \"Shh, Petty,\" her father said, \"Maxwell, we need to get some information, we have to find your master. When was the last time -\"\n[1754] Maxwell's cloudy eyes were full of suspicion. \"Who are you?\" [1755] The sergeant quickly produced his shiny badge, an old one he had not returned when he retired, which he used to assert his authority. Maxwell surrendered: \"Police!\" [1756] \"Answer my questions,\" Sam said sternly. \"When was the last time Dr. Allais was at home?\" [1757] \"I'm glad you came, sir,\" Maxwell murmured. \"I was worried, didn't know what to do. Dr. Allais is often out, but this is the longest he's been gone.\" [1758] \"All right, tell me, how long has he been gone?\" [1759] \"Let me think. Today is June 22nd. Oh, it's been over three weeks, sir. That was May 27th... Wait, sir, May 27th was a Monday. That's when Dr. Allais left home.\" [1760] \"The day the museum had its strange incident,\" Sam said quietly. [1761] \"Didn't I tell you already?\" Vera called out. [1762] Mr. Lane glanced around the living room and looked at Maxwell anxiously. He said slowly, \"Why don't you tell us what happened on May 27th, Maxwell? I think this story will be quite interesting.\" [1763] \"Well, sir, Dr. Aless left early in the morning and didn't come back until very late in the afternoon, almost evening. He\u2014\"\n[1764] \"How did he look?\" Rowe asked curiously, \"Excited?\" [1765] \"Yes, sir! Excited. He's usually a very reserved person who doesn't show any\u2014any emotions... Sir, you understand what I mean.\" [1766] \"Did he bring anything back?\" Roe's eyes lit up. [1767] \"Yes, sir. It looked like a book. But he was holding the same book in the morning, so\u2014\"\n[1768] \"How do you know it was the same book?\" [1769] Maxwell scratched his chin. \"Oh, they looked the same.\" [1770] The old gentleman said softly, \"That's all right. He took the 1606 Jagger out on Monday morning and came back with the 1599 Jagger, leaving the 1606 one there. Well\u2026 continue, Maxwell, what happened next?\" [1771] \"Next, sir, Dr. Aless told me before he left, 'Maxwell, I don't need you today, you can go home.' So I prepared his dinner and left\u2014walked down the alley to the road, took the bus back to Tarrytown. I live in Tarrytown, where there's family.\" [1772] \"Just that?\" Sam grumbled. [1773] The man looked dejected. \"I\u2014oh, sir, he told me before I left that he'd leave a package on the hallway the next morning, but he said not to mail it and to take it to Tarrytown and have the post office send it away on Tuesday morning. I came back from Tarrytown on Tuesday morning, Dr. Aless was, of course, not there, but the package was, so I took it to Tarrytown and mailed it as he instructed.\" [1774] \"What kind of package?\" Raine asked eagerly. [1775] Maxwell looked puzzled. \"What? Just a package, flat, I guess\u2014\"\n[1776] \"Was it a book?\" [1777] \"Right! It was shaped like a book, sir. It must have been a book.\" [1778] \"Let's take it one by one,\" the inspector said impatiently. \"Did Dr. Ales come back alone on Monday night? Did you notice anyone peeking around outside?\" [1779] \"Oh, he was alone.\" [1780] \"Did you see a burly Irishman, middle-aged, with a liver-colored face, wandering around? Did you see him?\" [1781] \"No, sir.\" [1782] \"Strange. Where did that damn old fellow hide himself?\" [1783] \"Don't forget, Dad,\" Percy said. \"Maxwell left soon after Alec came back. Maybe Donohue was hiding in the bushes, watching Maxwell leave, and then\u2014\"\n[1784] \"And then what?\" [1785] Percy sighed. \"Who knows? I'll give him a biscuit for that.\" [1786] \"Did you notice the address on the package?\" the young Rowe asked. [1787] \"Oh, yes, sir. This gentleman\u2026\" Maxwell nodded towards Lane, \"mentioned that name? The British Museum. It says New York City, Fifth Avenue and Sixty-fifth Street.\" [1788] \"Brown paper? Address written in blue ink?\" [1789] \"Yes, sir.\" [1790] Sam said, \"Well, that clears up a lot. No doubt the man in the blue hat was Alais. He stole the book, left the one from 1606, and then had it sent back to 1599 the next day by messenger.\" [1791] \"He's got nowhere to run,\" Vera said smugly. [1792] \"Good, good,\" Lane muttered, his eyebrows knitted, \"And, Maxwell, do you remember sending a similar package about two months ago?\" [1793] Maxwell's nerves were frayed by the theft of the book, and he became agitated, saying, \"I-I hope I haven't done anything wrong. I don't know... Yes, sir, I did send a similar package, to Mr. Crabbe, I believe, at the Saxon house on Fifth Avenue.\" [1794] \"Your eyesight's fine, isn't it?\" the sergeant said coldly. \"Well, Joe, your luck's in. You're off the hook.\" [1795] \"It's quite surprising,\" the young Rowe whispered. \"It seems everything revolves around Dr. Allis. He's causing trouble in Britain and inciting ruffians to raid the Saxon library. What kind of trick is this book playing on us?\" [1796] Joe Vera shrugged his thin shoulders, his beady eyes gleaming. He noticed the sergeant looking at him and tried to appear relaxed. The sergeant said gently, \"Joe, be smart and stay out of this. Alright, Maxwell, how long have you been working for Dr. Ales?\" [1797] Maxwell licked his dry lips. \"Oh, about three months. He came to Tarrytown... that was at the end of March... He placed an advertisement in the Tarrytown Times seeking an assistant. I applied and got the job. I know when he arrived because Jim Browning, the real estate agent who rented this house, is a friend of mine. Dr. Ales rented the house, paid six months' rent upfront, no contract, no prying, no character references. Browning said it's the way things are done... so we came here, like that. He... he was always good to me.\" [1798] \"No prying, huh?\" Perkins frowned. \"Next thing you know, we'll find out he's the Prince of Suriya, on a secret visit to the United States. Maxwell, does your charming host have many visitors?\" [1799] \"Oh, no, miss. No one comes... No, I'm mistaken. There was one person.\" [1800] \"Oh?\" Rine said softly. \"When?\" [1801] Maxwell furrowed his brow. \"He left a week before he left - I don't remember the exact day. It was a man, but his face was covered, and it was night, so I couldn't see his face. He wouldn't give his name, insisted on seeing Dr. Alles. I told him someone was waiting in the living room to see him, Dr. Alles was very excited, initially refused to come out. He still came out, went to the living room, stayed there for a while. He left, leaving the guest alone in the living room. He told me - I think he was nervous - that I didn't have to stay overnight, so I left. The next morning when I came back, he was gone.\u201d\n[1802] \"Did Dr. Alles ever mention this person, Maxwell? He never told you about this person?\" Rowe asked. [1803] \"Me? Sir,\u201d Maxwell chuckled. \"No, sir. Not a word.\u201d\n[1804] \"So who's this guy now?\" the inspector grumbled. \"Not this guy, Maxwell?\u201d He squeezed Vella's shoulder with his fat hand. [1805] Maxwell's eyes widened, and he burst out laughing again. \"Oh,