ate dining room of a downtown restaurant, the people at the table were all sorting out their jumbled thoughts. Krapf revealed the secret with a mocking, triumphant air, leaving everyone stunned for a moment. Hamnet Sedley was the mysterious Dr. Aless! Krapf gloated, licking his lips and sending them to the door. Their last glimpse of him saw him standing in the doorway of the Saxon mansion, framed by the Ionic columns, his hands rubbing together as if the legs of a cricket. He hunched his shoulders and tilted his head, as if saying, \"Well, you think your Dr. Sedley is your Dr. Aless, don't you? Old Krapf isn't a fool, is he? He exuded a sense of victory, as if he had satisfied his violent urges on a mob after executing a private lynching. [1309] Gordon Rowe had been preoccupied the whole time and now joined the small group, sitting quietly and watching the sunlight filter through the car window onto Percy's hair, but he seemed not to really see. [1310] \"There's one very odd thing,\" Mr. Philo Vance said as they sat down around the table, \"I admit I don't understand. That wretched old man, he was certainly impressive\u2014full of dramatic elements\u2014and what he said was all true. He's the kind of man who likes to tell the truth, especially when he knows it will hurt. But... Hamnet Sedley! Of course not.\" [1311] \"If Claver says his guest was Sedley,\" the young Rowe said dejectedly, \"then you can bet your boots it was Sedley.\" [1312] \"No, Gordon,\" Percy sighed. \"Sedley couldn't have been the one who visited Claver on May 6th. The board of the London Guildhall Museum held a farewell dinner for Dr. Ales on May 7th, so it couldn't have been him. Dr. Ales visited Claver in New York on May 6th. He's not a ghost; he couldn't have crossed the Atlantic in one night.\" [1313] \"Well, it's just too eerie. I know Claver, and I'll tell you, he's not lying. Every time he tells the truth and causes a stir, he's delighted, just like Mr. Vance said.\" [1314] \"Claver was absolutely certain,\" Percy said despairingly, poking at his roast. \"He swore the man was Sedley.\" [1315] \"What's the big deal?\" Inspector Belsey glared at Rowe. \"The old devil is lying, and that's that.\" [1316] \"Well,\" Raine said, \"he could be making up a story. These old bookworms can be jealous of each other's careers. Anyway, we're not getting anywhere with this. The whole thing is too bizarre...there's something I need to tell you about Dr. Allis.\" [1317] \"Oh, yes! You were about to tell us, but Klabber interrupted...isn't that name fictitious?\" [1318] \"Of course not! That's why it's so unusual, dear. Gordon, when you were at the Saxon's, you seemed to be on the verge of remembering something. Do you remember who Dr. Allis is now?\" [1319] \"I'm sorry, sir, I thought I remembered. Probably I came across the name while doing research.\" [1320] \"It's possible. I've never met Dr. Allis myself, and I know nothing about him personally, but I do know one thing. Unless this isn't a remarkable coincidence, such a person does exist, and he's a very clever and well-educated scholar of literature.\" The old gentleman thoughtfully chewed a stalk of celery, \"A few years ago\u2014oh, eight or ten years ago, there was an article in the Sturford Quarterly\u2014a journal devoted to bibliography\u2014\"\n[1321] \"Right!\" Rowe exclaimed, \"I used to receive it regularly when I was at university.\" [1322] \"That reminds you. The point is, the author of that article was Dr. Allais.\" [1323] \"A British magazine?\" Sam asked. [1324] \"Yes. I don't remember the specifics, but Dr. Allais discussed some ridiculous and lengthy controversies about Bacon, and some of the things he said greatly offended me. So, I wrote a long article in my own name to refute him, which was published in the quarterly. Dr. Allais was also quite angry and responded in the magazine's correspondence column. We went back and forth for several issues in the quarterly.\" [1325] \"I remember,\" Rowe said eagerly, his chin jutting forward, \"It was a heated debate. That's the guy, no doubt about it!\" [1326] \"Do you know where he lives?\" the Chief suddenly asked. [1327] \"Unfortunately, no.\" [1328] \"We can look up this magazine.\" [1329] \"I'm afraid not, Chief. Mr. Roche can tell you that The Stoke Quarterly went out of print five years ago.\" [1330] \"Damn it! Alright, I'll send another telegram to Trunch, asking him to look into it. You want\u2014\"\n[1331] \"By the way, Gordon,\" said the old gentleman, \"have you had a chance to check on the binding of the Jaggard edition of the 1599 folio we discussed? Any leads on a secret related to the binding?\" [1332] Rowe shrugged. \"Nothing so far. I did trace the binding back to about a hundred and fifty years ago\u2014it's not something a person would do. The current binding is at least that old, and as for the documents inside\u2014nothing so far. No leads yet.\" [1333] \"Hmm,\" said Lanyon, his eyes flashing, and then he resumed eating his salad. [1334] Percyns pushed his plate away impatiently. \"Oh, I can't chew it. This damned case is getting on my nerves. The whole business of Dr. Siddar being the Ales is so absurd, and yet it keeps going around in my head, and I can't get rid of it. Other things are so clear\u2014\"\n[1335] \"For example\u2014\" said the inspector skeptically. [1336] \"Dr. Aless's clues. Dad, you know that the man with the rainbow mustache who came to our office on May 6th was Dr. Aless.\" [1337] \"How did you come to that conclusion?\" the young Rowe whispered. [1338] \"He went to visit the Saxons that morning, took the library's stationery from them. He must have put on that ridiculous outfit in some hotel's washroom. He wrote down the symbols - annoying symbols! - put on his strange outfit, and went to Dad's office. These things are clear.\" [1339] \"It seems possible,\" said the old gentleman. [1340] \"He didn't think he'd be - killed,\" Percy bit his lip, \"he thought no one would know his secret, his million-dollar secret. Doesn't that sound stupid?... But he was a cunning man, he wouldn't take any risks. If he had called on the 20th and was fine, nothing would have happened, the envelope would still be sealed; if he hadn't called, we would have opened the envelope, seen the Saxon stationery, tracked down Klaber, and discovered this strange Dr. Aless - he must have told Klaber that fabricated story, so Klaber would remember - by the time we went looking for him, we would have known his name and his profession\u2026\"\n[1341] \"What an awful logical analysis!\" Young Rowe smiled slightly. [1342] \"So he asked you not to open the envelope unless I was there,\" Rene said quietly. \"He knew I would remember our debate in the quarterly; so I was brought in to confirm that Dr. All\u00e8s was a book collector.\" [1343] \"He must have planned it all from the start. If things didn't go well, like now, we'd have to look for Dr. All\u00e8s, a bookworm or something. How do we go about it?\" [1344] \"It's easy,\" the inspector said carelessly. \"Petti, that's my job. He said if he didn't call, it meant he was in trouble, right? That means we know where he disappeared from, apart from his physical appearance, name, profession, or occupation - he must have been mixed up somewhere or killed.\" [1345] \"Well said, Inspector,\" Reth murmured. \"Right on the mark. You must collect all the police reports of murders, kidnappings, or disappearances from May 20th - the day he didn't call on time - to a few days ago.\" [1346] The sergeant growled, \"I know, I know. Do you know how much work that is?\" [1347] \"Not as bad as you think, Sergeant. What you're looking for is clear. Percy has already said it.\" [1348] \"Alright!\" Sam said glumly. \"I'll do it. But what's in it for me? I have to live, you know.\" [1349] Mr. J. B. Rathbone sent Sam the sergeant back to the office, dropped Miss Samantha Percy and Gordon Rowe off at Central Park, and signaled to Dromio, then sat back in the car, deep in thought. Now no one was watching him, he sat quietly in the back seat, clutching his cane handle tightly, staring at the back of Dromio's head, his face a picture of shifting emotions. Unlike most old men, he was not accustomed to talking to himself aloud, possibly because his deaf ear had not made him prone to such habits. He thought things out in pictures, some of which were so bizarre that he had to close his eyes to see them more clearly. [1350] The Lincoln limousine swiftly drove uptown, heading towards Westchester. [1351] After a long time, the old man opened his eyes and saw the lush trees and winding driveway leading to the garage. He leaned forward and lightly tapped Dromio's shoulder. [1352] \"Dromio, didn't I tell you to go to Dr. Martini's first?\" [1353] Dromio, the faithful driver, turned his head slightly so that his master could see his lips. \"Mr. Vance, is there an emergency? Are you feeling unwell again?\" [1354] The old gentleman smiled. \"No, I'm fine. This visit is purely out of scientific interest.\" [1355] \"Oh.\" Dromio scratched his left ear, shrugged, and pressed down on the accelerator. [1356] He parked the car in front of a house near Ephraim. The house was half-hidden in the trees, with vines and late-blooming roses growing outside. A silver-haired, imposing man sat on the porch smoking a pipe. [1357] \"Ah, Martini,\" Reine stretched his legs. \"Lucky you're home at this time.\" [1358] The imposing man widened his eyes. \"Mr. Reine, what brings you here? Please come in, please come in.\" [1359] Reine chuckled and closed the door behind him. \"Don't be surprised, old man. I'm feeling great.\" They shook hands, and Dr. Martini's tired eyes looked at him professionally. \"You look good, don't you?\" [1360] \"Excellent. How's your heart?\" [1361] \"It's beating merrily. I can't say the same for my stomach.\" [1362] They entered the doctor's house. A fluffy dog sniffed at Lane's ankle and then walked away indifferently. \"I don't understand, at my age\u2014\"\n[1363] \"Dear great man, a lifetime of theater menus doesn't do wonders for digestion. Sit down. I managed to sneak away from the hospital for a few hours. The hospital is boring, and I haven't come across an interesting case.\" [1364] Lane smiled. \"I have one for you.\" [1365] Dr. Martini took the pipe out of his mouth. \"Ah, I think I know. Not yourself, I presume?\" [1366] \"No, no.\" [1367] \"A troublesome matter?\" Martini said with a half-smile. \"I'll give up the afternoon countryside fun.\" [1368] \"No need.\" The old man leaned forward. \"This case \u2013 I believe \u2013 can be diagnosed while sitting in a chair.\" He suddenly looked around. \"Martini, I think you should close the door.\" [1369] The doctor was puzzled and then stood up, shutting the sunlight outside. [1370] \"You're mysterious,\" he said, sitting back in the chair with the pipe in his mouth. \"Confidential? I guess it's a criminal case, but there's no one here to listen \u2013\"\n[1371] Laine's gaze was stern. \"Martini, when a person goes deaf, they feel like the walls have ears. Old friend, I've gotten involved in this most incredible event, with many branches at every point\u2026\"\n[1372] Dromio, dozing at the wheel, swatted a bee that had landed on his collar, somewhat surprised. The sweet fragrance of roses intoxicated him. Martini's door had been closed for half an hour and now opened, revealing his tall figure. Dromio heard Martini say lightly, \"It's probably the only way, Mr. Laine. I must see the paper before I can give you an opinion. But even so, as I said\u2026\"\n[1373] \"You scientists!\" Dromio heard Laine's tone of impatience. \"I used to hope the problem would be clearer. But\u2026\" He shrugged and extended his hand. \"Thank you for showing interest. I think I deserve some credit. I'll bring the paper to you tonight.\" [1374] \"Ah, yes, I'll come to Hamlet's Manor tonight.\" [1375] \"Oh, nonsense! That would be too much trouble for you. I'll come here again\u2026\"\n[1376] \"Don't say that. Driving does me good, and I want to see old Quincannon. The last time I saw him, his arteries were in a bad state.\" [1377] Dromio didn't understand what was going on and opened the door. His master quickly walked down the path, stopped, and raised his eyebrows sharply, saying sharply, \"Did you see anyone lurking around here?\" [1378] Dromio was startled. \"Lurking, sir? Philo?\" [1379] \"Yes, did you see anyone else?\" [1380] Dromio scratched his ear. \"I probably dozed off for a minute or two, sir, but I don't think\u2026\"\n[1381] \"Ah, Dromio Jr.,\" the old gentleman sighed, climbing into the car, \"when will you learn to be more alert... I suppose it doesn't matter. He waved cheerfully to Dr. Martini, \"Pull over at the gas station, Dromio Jr., to the telegraph office.\" [1382] The car drove away. When they reached the gas station, Dromio Jr. found the telegraph office, and Philo Roen walked in. He glanced at the clock on the wall, then sat down at a small table, reaching for a stack of yellow paper and a chained pencil. For a few seconds, he stared at the pencil tip. The pencil was sharp, but he didn't notice. His eyes were focused on something else.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."